@article{ WOS:001615369800001,
Author = {Gonzalez, Ezequiel and Bikic, Boris Oliver and Fabian, Diego and Sanchez
   Dominguez, Maria Virginia and Fenoglio, Maria Silvina},
Title = {Soil- and ground-dwelling arthropod diversity on green roofs: Functional
   groups are strongly influenced by substrate depth and plant community},
Journal = {ECOLOGICAL ENTOMOLOGY},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {51},
Number = {2},
Pages = {356-370},
Month = {APR},
Abstract = {Green roofs are a nature-based solution with the potential to support
   urban arthropod biodiversity. However, evidence remains inconclusive, as
   many roof characteristics and arthropod groups are still understudied.
   We used a multi-taxa and functional trait approach focused on soil- and
   ground-dwelling arthropod communities to assess how green roof features
   related to structure and design, plant community, management and
   surrounding landscape influence the species richness and abundance of
   arthropods (overall and of groups defined by vertical distribution, body
   size, mobility and feeding habits). We sampled arthropods (N = 7155, 244
   species) using pitfall traps in 20 green roofs from central Argentina,
   and recorded green roof characteristics (i.e., size, age, height,
   substrate depth and composition, plant species richness and composition,
   irrigation and landscape green cover). Substrate depth was the most
   important structural variable, being positively linked to the species
   richness of euedaphic and hemiedaphic arthropods and other groups.
   Additionally, substrates containing plant litter and sand benefitted
   detritivores. Plant composition influenced the richness and abundance of
   herbivores and parasitoids, probably due to the presence of specific
   resources. Irrigation increased total abundance and richness of
   hemiedaphics and small arthropods, although total richness was
   unaffected. Landscape green cover was positively related to parasitoid
   and small-sized arthropod abundance, highlighting the role of green
   spaces as sources of individuals. Our findings demonstrate that green
   roofs can support diverse communities of soil- and ground-dwelling
   arthropods with varied functional traits, offering practical insights
   for designing biodiverse green roofs and conserving species within urban
   ecosystems.
   Los techos verdes son una soluci \& oacute;n basada en la naturaleza,
   con el potencial de sostener la biodiversidad de artr \& oacute;podos
   urbanos. Sin embargo, la evidencia es a \& uacute;n poco concluyente, ya
   que muchas caracter \& iacute;sticas de los techos y grupos de artr \&
   oacute;podos a \& uacute;n siguen siendo poco estudiados. Utilizamos un
   enfoque multi-taxon \& oacute;mico y basado en rasgos funcionales,
   centrado en comunidades de artr \& oacute;podos de suelo y caminadores,
   para evaluar c \& oacute;mo las caracter \& iacute;sticas de techos
   verdes relacionadas a la estructura y al dise \& ntilde;o, la comunidad
   de plantas, el manejo y el paisaje circundante influyen sobre la riqueza
   de especies y abundancia de artr \& oacute;podos (total y por grupos
   definidos por su distribuci \& oacute;n vertical, tama \& ntilde;o
   corporal, movilidad y h \& aacute;bitos alimenticios). Muestreamos artr
   \& oacute;podos (N = 7155, 244 species) utilizando trampas de ca \&
   iacute;da en 20 techos verdes en el centro de Argentina, y registramos
   caracter \& iacute;sticas de los techos verdes (i.e., tama \& ntilde;o,
   edad, altura, profundidad y composici \& oacute;n del sustrato, irrigaci
   \& oacute;n y cobertura verde en el paisaje). La profundidad del
   sustrato fue la variable estructural m \& aacute;s importante, estando
   positivamente relacionada con la riqueza de especies de artr \&
   oacute;podos eued \& aacute;ficos y hemied \& aacute;ficos y de otros
   grupos. Adicionalmente, sustratos que contuvieron fibras vegetales y
   arena beneficiaron a detrit \& iacute;voros. La composici \& oacute;n de
   plantas influenci \& oacute; la riqueza y abundancia de herb \&
   iacute;voros y parasitoides, probablemente debido a la presencia de
   recursos espec \& iacute;ficos. La irrigaci \& oacute;n increment \&
   oacute; la abundancia total y la riqueza de artr \& oacute;podos hemied
   \& aacute;ficos y peque \& ntilde;os, aunque la riqueza total no fue
   afectada. La cobertura verde en el paisaje se relacion \& oacute;
   positivamente con la abundancia de parasitoides y artr \& oacute;podos
   peque \& ntilde;os, destacando el rol de los espacios verdes como
   fuentes de individuos. Nuestros hallazgos demuestran que los techos
   verdes pueden sostener a comunidades diversas de artr \& oacute;podos de
   suelo y caminadores con rasgos funcionales variados, lo que ofrece
   informaci \& oacute;n pr \& aacute;ctica para el dise \& ntilde;o de
   techos verdes biodiversos y la conservaci \& oacute;n de especies dentro
   de ecosistemas urbanos.},
